WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:09.280
On the mainframe, the big three that immediately come to mind are Kix, IMS, and DB2.

2
00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:16.280
When I first started working on Z-Systems, I heard these said together so often that I thought it was two things, Kix, IMS, and DB2.

3
00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:18.280
But we're talking about three components here.

4
00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:26.280
There's Kix, or C-I-C-S, spelled C-I-C-S, IMS, and DB2. Three different things.

5
00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:28.280
Let's start with Kix.

6
00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:36.280
Kix has been around for a long time, and it powers many of the world's transactions, such as ATMs, managing insurance policies,

7
00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:44.280
utility bills, retail, shopping, and others. And it does it all securely and reliably day in and day out.

8
00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:51.280
A Kix transaction can consist of many facets, including delivering the user interface, retrieving and updating information,

9
00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:55.280
and communicating with other applications and network-attached devices.

10
00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:02.280
In Kix, a transaction typically lasts a very short time and is associated with one unit of work.

11
00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:08.280
Kix takes the work to be processed and lines it up with the program or programs required to complete it.

12
00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:14.280
This is the job of the transaction manager to ensure the work completes successfully according to the process

13
00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:18.280
and that there's nothing left hanging out there when we tell the user that it's complete.

14
00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:21.280
Here's what's under the hood that lets this all happen.

15
00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:27.280
A Kix system manages an area of storage called a region, which lives in an address space.

16
00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:30.280
You'll hear that term a lot, a Kix region.

17
00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:36.280
A Kix region uses domains, which contain management nodes for transaction processing.

18
00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:42.280
Now, a single region can be defined to perform all the functions necessary to process a transaction.

19
00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:50.280
Typically, a Kix installation will have several different regions, each one devoted to a different type of transaction.

20
00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:52.280
Think of it like an auto repair center.

21
00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:57.280
This area is for bodywork, over here is for transmission issues, tires and wheels over there.

22
00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:03.280
That way, all the tools that are needed are close by and the area isn't crowded with stuff that they don't need.

23
00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:09.280
There are three types of domains that supervise and control transaction processing.

24
00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:13.280
Remember, these are the domains within the region.

25
00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:17.280
We've covered a lot there, so let's make sure we've got the basic order right.

26
00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:25.280
A Kix system manages an area of storage called a blank, and within that, it uses blanks.

27
00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:28.280
Let's see if you remember what those are called.

28
00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:37.280
So, a Kix system manages areas of storage called regions, and then within the regions, it manages domains.

29
00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:46.280
There's the transaction manager, the XM, which is responsible for receiving transaction requests and creating the organizing tasks to process them.

30
00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:54.280
Then there's the program manager, or PG, which handles locating and invoking applications required for processing transactions.

31
00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:57.280
And then there's the storage manager, SM.

32
00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:03.280
This domain is responsible for allocating memory resources required for all of this to happen.

33
00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:06.280
You can see in this diagram, we have three types of regions.

34
00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:12.280
There's the TOR, terminal owning region, which handles connections to terminals and other remote devices.

35
00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:19.280
Then there's the AOR for application owning region, and the DOR for data owning region.

36
00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:22.280
The glue that connects these regions is VTAM.

37
00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:27.280
Now, we haven't really spent much time talking about VTAM yet, and we're not going to get deep into it.

38
00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:31.280
Just know that it has to do with networking, and it's part of comm server.

39
00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:38.280
The AORs and DORs talk with other applications like databases and user written applications.

40
00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:41.280
One of those databases could be IMS.

41
00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:43.280
Now, what's IMS?

42
00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:50.280
IMS, Information Management System, has a long and interesting history as well, which includes some development done for NASA.

43
00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:58.280
What makes IMS unique is that it has, or is, depending on how you look at it, a hierarchical database management system.

44
00:03:58.280 --> 00:04:05.280
It is widely used for manufacturing type applications, but you'll see it in just about any industry.

45
00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:08.280
IMS consists of three components.

46
00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:12.280
One is the database manager, that's the IMS DB.

47
00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:16.280
Then there's the transaction manager, that's the IMS TM.

48
00:04:16.280 --> 00:04:22.280
And then there's the system services that provide common services to both the DB and the TM.

49
00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:28.280
These work together to deliver data and transactional capabilities in an extremely efficient way.

50
00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:35.280
For more complex data handling needs, there's DB2, Big D, little b, 2.

51
00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:47.280
DB2 is a database, and it offers exceptional availability, massive scalability, flexibility and installation across platforms and locations, and a low total cost of ownership.

52
00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:54.280
DB2 is considered to be a relational database, so data exists in one or more constructs known as tables.

53
00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:57.280
And within those tables, there are columns and rows.

54
00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:03.280
And you interact with data by using the Structured Query Language, or SQL.

55
00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:07.280
Though you can also use DB2 for unstructured data and so much more.

56
00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:10.280
Remember those ACID properties we talked about before?

57
00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:14.280
DB2 people take each one of those very seriously.

58
00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:20.280
They won't chase something that gives them increased availability if it means giving up consistency and vice versa.

59
00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:30.280
When it comes to truly enterprise applications, the middleware like Kix, IMS and DB2 need to step up and deliver time after time after time after time.

60
00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:33.280
Can't stop, won't stop.

61
00:05:34.280 --> 00:05:35.280
Transaction.
